Subject: [PATCH] feat(bedrock): manage Character Creator characters

Read the five account recipes and native Dressing Room catalog so players can select a character, browse owned pieces, and edit saved recipes. Use the native appearance PUT flow with a profile hash check and a readback for slot, piece, color, size, cape, and emote changes. Keep the settings profile and unrelated recipe fields intact.

Create new characters from the nine bundled Bedrock 1.26.51 starter recipes, or copy an existing recipe into an empty slot. The bundled definitions supply complete default piece IDs and colors, so matching catalog pieces can be equipped without another saved character. Delete a character by saving its base-only recipe. Both operations follow captured native request shapes; deletion keeps another saved character active. The four-position emote wheel uses saved recipe entries and native empty placeholders.

Bedrock 1.26.51 cape captures on the DressingRoom_Capes Store page and the appearance PUT flow showed that an equipped cape uses the catalog pack UUID without a piece suffix and sits before the four emote entries. A direct authenticated account test confirmed that equipping a cape adds one mesh and texture to the assembled GLTF model; the account was restored afterward.

Sequential native Size captures confirmed four fixed height entry pairs. Changing height removes the previous pair and saves the selected pair before the emote wheel. A separate arm-width capture changed only the base arm field from wide to slim while leaving cs_arm as wide. The account was restored to its original recipe after these captures.

The account API returns recipes rather than a Bedrock skin packet. Build the active character from licensed skeleton, body, face and equipped piece assets when the complete recipe can resolve locally. Keep the service GLTF path for unresolved free packs while their content keys remain unavailable. Persist the assembled geometry and atlas for login and refresh them after active character edits. Resolve persona handles and tint groups from the complete recipe and current catalog, persist them, and include them in login claims. Built-in UUID types come from the native 1.26.51 Store pages and default pack metadata; height body and skeleton UUIDs match native Size captures.

A native 1.26.51 Mojang Office Shirt equip saved its owned pack UUID with no suffix. The login used that UUID as both PieceId and PackId, and the Store product UUID as ProductId, rather than the encrypted pack metadata piece_id. Free catalog pieces use /f, marketplace emotes use /e, and built-in pieces and emotes use /d and /de respectively. Preserve saved colors on category swaps and insert new categories before the emote wheel. The profile hash check and exact readback still protect each edit. Raw receipts, account profiles, keys, and captures stay private.

Decode owned persona assets using the current account's inventory receipt,
PlayFab GetPublishedItem, and the native libhttpclient CDN user agent.
Bound archive sizes and validate each manifest/content ID before decrypting.
Receipts and content keys stay in memory. Match animated geometry to the
character's body and arm sizes, replace its static preview surfaces, and
assemble looping strips for persistence, login, and live skin updates.

Private Bedrock 1.26.51 captures verified the owned Office Shirt pack and
the HelliArm's 16-frame strip and ten arm cubes. Keep those assets and the
account receipt outside the exported patch. Unresolved free pack keys
remain separate work; those recipes still use the model service for their
base appearance.

ModelBinary places the Steve face texture on a +Z head surface, while the
Bedrock 1.26.51 login capture binds that same face region to the -Z normal.
Reflect model positions, node translations, and normals across Z and reverse
triangle winding when exporting skin geometry. This preserves native skin
coordinates in both Java previews and packets instead of turning only the
menu camera.

Native Bedrock 1.26.51 HTTPS captures equipped HelliArm on the right and
then left. Appearance PUT saved separate pack UUID /r and /l entries;
choosing the same pack on both sides did not merge them into a bare ID.
Use those recipes for owned side editing, preserve the opposite side and
all tint metadata, and retain the emote wheel ordering. Packet handles use
LeftArm/RightArm and LeftLeg/RightLeg from the target protocol enum.
Filter animated bones through their limb ancestry so the unselected side
keeps its static geometry. Tests cover arms, legs, tint retention, separate
entries, removal, and side-specific geometry. The owned-arm flow has native
capture evidence; the analogous leg flow still needs a native write capture.
Independent execution of the target recipe encoder verifies free and built-in
side flags; preserve their source flag during side changes.

Keep side selection visible at the default GUI scale. Compact wardrobes
use Minecraft's category cycle button, smaller thumbnails, and a single
row of emote positions; larger windows retain the full category list.
Reflow visible footer actions so hidden buttons do not truncate labels.

Load owned emotes through the same receipt-authorized pack path. Use their
pack UUID for the protocol identity and their named animation
source for playback. A Dressing Room preview plays on the active account
character without changing its recipe, with replay and stop controls. Keep
the native root/waist/body hierarchy in menu models and apply the same pose
to animated clothing surfaces. Reset authored poses before every frame.

Use the shared core float evaluator for actor-local Molang variables and
frame queries, with bounded AST size and nested-loop execution. Preserve
native float loop remainders and the shared host execution budget. Interpolate pre/post discontinuities,
linear and Catmull-Rom tracks, blend weights, scale, and animation clocks.
Creator animation schemas and Blockbench's interpolation implementation
guide the sampler. Effects and timelines fail explicitly until their playback exists.
Entity-relative rotation uses the authored bone hierarchy. Owned native 1.26.51 Battle Cry and
Kadoosh metadata resolve to 6.5 and 4.75 seconds; private asset tests sample
every frame at 60 Hz. A Java GUI recording verifies visible Battle Cry motion, attached clothing,
completion reset, and readable Replay/Stop controls at the default scale.
The fixture is offline; live account downloads still need verification.

Native 1.26.51 EMOTE and EMOTE_LIST captures confirm the pack UUID on the
wire, not metadata piece_id. Battle Cry sends 130 ticks and flags zero.
Walking clears PlayerAuthInput Emoting immediately; completion clears it
after 130 ticks. Expose those packets through the ViaBedrock provider.

Add a rebindable four-position in-world wheel with mouse and 1-4 controls.
Cache only entitled animations and advertise loaded wheel entries. Apply
one sampled actor pose to body and clothing through the authored hierarchy;
reset on movement, completion, disconnect, and account changes.

Native Bedrock 1.26.51 catalog responses also serve personabinary from
content1.prod.catalog.playfab.com. Fetch those official PlayFab content
hosts as well as xforgeassets hosts. Require HTTPS with no credentials,
fragment or nonstandard port, and keep authorization on service requests.
The add-on obtains keys from the current receipt and decrypts in memory;
it never looks up a native installation, external key file or extractor.

Ship the matching Bedrock 1.26.51.1 built-in persona and stable vanilla
libraries as versioned resource archives. Verify the protocol version,
archive checksums, paths and resource bounds before loading the shared
library. Built-in models, images, emotes, sounds and particles load without
an account, network access or a writable cache. Remove Store sign-in,
package acquisition and native helper binaries from the client and CI.

The PistonDecompiler checkout contains only the Windows executable.
Resource bytes come from the matching installed 1.26.51.1 package. Its
projection matches every file in the previous licensed resource cache.
Pass the resource directory through bedrockBuiltinAssets so native payloads
stay outside exported source patches. Keep native copyright notices.

Decode PNG face strips and BGRA TGA tint masks alongside geometry sources.
Filter unused body variants before decoding equipped animated textures.
Load the four default emotes for preview and world playback locally.
Compose equipped skin, mouth, eyes, facial hair, and hair face sources in
native piece order, repeat static layers across animated frames, and bind
extracted head and hat meshes to the resulting animated face surface.
Bedrock 1.26.51.1 native compositor observations establish four-channel
weighted tint transfer with HSL, LCh, and luminance contributions. Bind
alpha-mask colors to the character skin tone, preserve source alpha, and
truncate output channels as the native client does. Apply the same tint
math to animated body pieces, retaining independent limb recipe colors.

A private two-frame native face capture matches all 2,048 RGBA pixels.
Isolated native color transfer evaluations match 512 deterministic input
vectors. Targeted tests cover compositing, recipe colors, independent limb
tiles, and geometry bindings. Normalize null optional transforms in native
geometry exports before assembly. A private Java GUI recording verifies
open and closed composed face frames alongside animated arm frames.
Research binaries, captured images, assets,
and native execution harnesses stay outside the exported patch.

Native archive format reference:
https://github.com/bedrock-crustaceans/brarchive/blob/main/FORMAT.md

Build static body geometry directly from the selected skeleton and body
zone sources. Empty arm_size selects the shared variant. Preserve pose
bones and locators when an equipped piece replaces a zone, including the
bare arm and clothing zones replaced by HelliArm. Select independent
recipe sides before decoding textures. Join polygon layers by offsetting
vertex, normal and UV indices independently instead of rejecting a shared
bone. Pack static surfaces with the native one-pixel duplicated border.

Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 clothing maps use compressed BGRA TGA pixels whose
red and green channels encode offsets around 128. An opaque layer clears
the mapped underlying texel when its source has no coverage at that texel.
Apply this coverage rule before the native tint compositor. A private
Steve/HelliArm capture matches all 16,384 body RGBA pixels and body mesh
UVs. Tests assemble all nine starters across four heights and two arm
widths, validate the resulting geometry parser bindings, and combine the
locally assembled body with the blinking face and ten animated arm cubes.
More overlapping outfits and texture resolutions still need comparison.

Extract the base vanilla model archives through the same verified package
and license flow. Refresh the cache schema so old persona-only caches do
not silently omit geometry parents. Resolve classic imports lazily from
the licensed library on a worker, preserving local pack definitions and
keeping account changes or closed screens from opening stale results.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1 licensed acquisition returns 229 files including both
model archives. Tests resolve the seven legacy skin-model chains across
the global model files, reuse the cache, and refresh the older schema.
Versioned resource-pack overlays and native visual comparisons remain
separate verification work. No game assets or credentials are bundled.

Load the matching player client definition, animations, and controllers
from the same licensed vanilla package. Cache format 3 refreshes earlier
model-only caches before actor compilation; a licensed test extracts 232
files. Resolve skin aliases over the player defaults, including deliberate
empty aliases, and validate the reachable graph before import completes.

Run initialization and per-frame scripts with actor-local variables.
Preserve ordered weighted references, nested controller states, first
matching transitions, entry/exit scripts, temporary remapped variables,
and independent animation clocks. Native player controllers contain float
suffixes; normalize them outside quoted values. Supply typed item queries
and string equality because numeric equality collapses distinct item IDs.

Play classic aliases in previews and world models through the same authored
hierarchy as emotes. Entity-relative rotations cancel parent animation
rotation before flattening attachments. Private matching package tests
establish zombie-arm alias overrides and the additive riding pose. Tests
cover nested variable restoration, controller re-entry, typed queries,
and simultaneous parent rotations.

Controller crossfades, completion queries, effects, full item-action query
bindings, geometry-provided alias precedence, and native visual comparisons
remain incomplete. Preserve explicit errors for unsupported controller
fields rather than silently claiming full controller support.

Reference:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/documents/animations/animationcontroller?view=minecraft-bedrock-stable

Resolve equipped persona capes separately from packet piece handles. Download
and decrypt their packs using the same account receipt and PlayFab catalog
flow, then bind the selected texture and tint mask to geometry.cape from
the licensed vanilla model library. Translate only the cape surface bones
by the equipped body's pivot offset, retaining the height skeleton.
Normalize box UVs to explicit faces before scaling and packing the atlas.
The native bottom face retains its U orientation while V reverses.

Authenticated Bedrock 1.26.51.1 model-service comparisons with Copper Cape
establish the attachment at all four heights. Tests compare rendered rest
vertices, normals and UVs, preserve every cape texel, and round-trip the
assembled character through account persistence and the skin codec.
Additional tests cover mirrored and higher-resolution texture mappings,
ambiguous recipes, and separation from persona packet claims. Account
recipes were restored after research. These comparisons establish static
bindings; cape motion and a native multiplayer comparison remain pending.

Resolve freely available persona packs with unique content keys decoded from
the current account receipt when their pack UUID has no matching entry.
Authenticated 1.26.51.1 research decrypted both Asymmetric Button Up and
A-Line hair this way, although neither pack appeared in the receipt.
Retain native catalog redemption eligibility and verify the published
product identity, zero price, persona type and pack UUID before trying
shared keys. Paid or redemption-required pieces still need their own
entitlement. No game installation, copied key or external extractor enters
the production flow. Empty-key receipts remain an unresolved case.

Private tests assemble both unowned free pieces with licensed starter
assets. The model service's clothing image matches all 16,384 body texels.
Independent target-game compositor execution matches all 2,048 face pixels,
including translucent hair. The service's static face differs at twelve
translucent edge pixels; retain game composition rather than changing tint
math to match that service discrepancy. Account recipes were restored.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1 accepts multiple emote animation-source declarations but
selects the first entry. Its loader checks for a nonempty 64-byte source
vector, resolves the first name, and loads only that entry's file. The
piece validator does not restrict the source count. Accept these lists
and preserve their order instead of rejecting the entire emote. Missing
first sources remain errors; later declarations do not supply a fallback.
This behavior comes from focused inspection of the matching native loader
and validator. Tests cover source ordering, sampled poses, missing files,
and empty lists. All native binaries and exports remain private.

Implement start and loop delays in the shared bone sampler from focused
Bedrock 1.26.51.1 clock inspection and independent execution. The native
player evaluates start delay once, consumes wait time before its custom
clock, retains the full delta at exact expiry, and wraps only after crossing
the last loop frame. Its completed flag persists, so loop delay reevaluates
on every subsequent active update, including updates within later cycles.
Preserve these target behaviors rather than imposing different semantics
from the general Creator description. Native update, reset, and completion
routines are FUN_141e68a20, FUN_141e69a60, and FUN_141e76b30.

Reuse the sampled pose for repeated elapsed-time passes. Stop previews from
playback state and preserve an incoming emote packet's duration without
truncating it to the asset length. Ordinary tracks stop after the final
frame; held tracks retain that frame. Tests compare isolated native clock
execution and cover delayed activation, expiry, loop boundaries, custom
clocks, blend suspension, repeated passes, held poses, and restart.
Timeline execution, sounds, particles, and controller completion queries
remain separate work. Private binaries and harnesses remain outside Git.

Execute Molang timeline scripts after each bone sample with the track's
current clock and effective frame delta. Bedrock 1.26.51.1 normalizes
entries with a stable timestamp sort, tests previous < event <= current,
and resets the event cursor when the clock wraps past a loop boundary.
It does not replay the outgoing tail or skipped whole cycles. Timeline
writes remain actor-local and reach later tracks in the same update.
Named actor events, sounds, and particles still report unsupported data.
Independent execution of the licensed target's timeline normalizer and
animation player verifies ordering, delays, loop boundaries, held frames,
custom clocks, and bone-before-event sampling with synthetic inputs.

Port controller completion and transition blending from Bedrock
1.26.51.1 (protocol 2193). Independent execution of the target query
handlers, controller updater, version comparator, and Euler blend helper
establishes completion ordering, empty states, persistent loop flags,
state clocks, crossfade weights, and the negative 180-degree tie.

Retain each controller resource's format version. The target constructor
uses the 1.18.10 boundary for immediate initial transitions versus initial
state entry. Keep the older licensed player graph's posture behavior.
Outgoing states continue updating during blends. A completed fade clears
its outgoing state clock after sampling the incoming state. Nested
controller resets retain their selected state, and self-transitions do
not repeat entry or exit scripts. Zero-weight players freeze. Duplicate
render passes reuse the pose; lifetime rewind creates fresh actor state.

Private native fixtures compare controller clocks and sampled child
weights, including a fade completed on its transition frame. Other
fixtures compare shortest-path position, rotation, scale composition, and
format-version initialization. Targeted tests cover re-entry, nested
completion, query binding cleanup, zero weights, and repeated frames.
Research executables, licensed assets, and probe outputs remain private.
Native visual motion, mixed rotation spaces, and constant-only animation
length defaults remain unverified.

Assemble animated shared body textures from equipped assets instead of
requiring the static model service. Independent Bedrock 1.26.51.1
execution verifies native 32/128 texture groups, square source frames,
power-of-two lengths, horizontal columns, and shorter-strip repetition.
Replace the previous LCM atlas with the target compositor's grouping.

Compose static and animated clothing layers in native piece order with
frame-specific tint masks. Preserve clothing-map offsets across frame
boundaries. Independent compositor output matches all 32,768 pixels of a
synthetic two-frame body, including tint changes and translucent pixels.
Keep static attachments and capes separate; merge body and geometry
strips into the corresponding animated group. Rectangular polygon UVs
use each atlas dimension independently. Private native probes and assets
remain outside the patch. Native equipped-outfit comparisons are pending.

Expose mouth and facial hair palettes in their Dressing Room categories.
Independent execution of the Bedrock 1.26.51.1 palette initializer and
picker selection verifies mouth channel two, facial hair channel zero,
and the exposed swatches. Preserve the other recipe channels and reject
mismatched piece categories. Premium piece color controls remain pending.

Support independent free and built-in limb recipes from Bedrock 1.26.51.1
(protocol 2193). Independent execution of native FUN_14142d750 confirms
owned /l and /r, free /fl and /fr, and default /dl and /dr. The source flag
precedes the side flag. Switching to both sides retains /f or /d, while
owned pieces return to a bare UUID. Native enums distinguish legs 19-21
and arms 22-24. Executables and execution harnesses stay private.

Enable free limb controls without requiring a redemption or paid
entitlement. Preserve the opposite side, separate colors, recipe fields,
and wheel positions when switching between owned and free pieces. Decode
the combined flags for packet handles and local static and animated
geometry selection. Reject unknown flags rather than discarding them.
Tests compare all 18 native encodings and numeric piece types, side-change
round trips, mixed-source replacement, removal, and animated limb tints.
Fresh native leg HTTPS write and multiplayer visual comparisons remain
pending.

Download account-owned classic packs from the native
MultiItemPage_PersonaSkinSelector Store page. Bedrock 1.26.51.1 captures
provide product IDs, purchased ownership, and skinpack UUID/version
identities. Current authenticated research resolves Birdie Wings 1.0.5
and Earth Skin 1.0.1 through PlayFab GetPublishedItem, skinbinary content,
and official CDN downloads using libhttpclient/1.0.0.0. Native downloads
wrap their encrypted archives in spack0.zip or a named skin-pack ZIP.

Share inventory receipt decoding, catalog lookup, bounded HTTPS downloads,
and pack decryption across persona, emote, and classic content. Classic
downloads require their exact account receipt key and unchanged product,
pack, and version identities. Never try unrelated receipt keys for paid
classic packs. Keys and receipts stay in memory; native installations and
copied keys are not part of the production flow.

Read local and downloaded classic packs through one bounded in-memory
reader, then reuse their model, options, preview, per-account selection,
and skin transport. Preserve null root parents as omitted optional fields
as used by Earth Skin. The native asset supplies regression evidence.
Keep the account pack list mounted during downloads and reject results
after closing the screen, switching accounts, or changing the selection.
Arrange the six Dressing Room actions in two readable rows.

Private licensed fixtures verify both wrappers, decrypted textures, custom
geometry, saved selection, and login skin data. Targeted tests retain
ownership distinctions, repeated-item deduplication, product/version
validation, and receipt identity checks. Downloaded assets, credentials,
and research probes remain private. Purchase, redemption, remote classic
recipe synchronization, and broader native visual comparisons remain
pending.

Validate the Store page's version against the published product, not the
pack manifest version. The current Earth Skin publication uses 1.0.1,
while its downloaded manifest declares 1.0.0. Comparing those different
version domains would reject a valid owned pack.

Honor metadata allow_tint_override during palette selection. Use authored
base colors when no selected tint exists, and preserve fixed piece colors
instead of replacing them with recipe channels. Resolve other pieces'
alpha tint from the equipped skin's selected red channel, including a
fixed skin tint. Missing skin assets preserve the piece alpha tint. Skin
pieces use the slot tone on all channels only when overrides are allowed;
capes bypass selected tint in the target assembler.

Independent execution of Bedrock 1.26.51.1 FUN_141490e10 verifies 448
palette-selection combinations. The probe supplies decoded colors at
metadata accessor boundaries and executes the native selection branches,
including recipe lookup, fixed skin colors, missing defaults, and capes.
This evidence establishes palette selection, not premium UI availability.

Load pack-local emote announcements and alternate messages. Preserve
Bedrock 1.26.51.1 actor substitution, highlight and escape order, float
cooldowns, and fifth-repeat alternate selection. Independent execution
of FUN_1447677d0 and FUN_1402046e0 establishes these rules. Native outgoing
flags remain zero, while incoming bit 2 and StartGame emote-chat muting
suppress announcements. Ignore local echoes as the native handler does.

Expose local emote-chat muting and use the Java client's chat and blocked
player checks. Native platform-specific communication filtering and live
multiplayer announcement comparison remain pending. Keep licensed text,
execution fixtures, and research probes private.

Keep unresolved classic animation and controller tracks inactive without
marking them complete. Native execution of the target animation and
controller players verifies missing handles and missing definitions,
